\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 An NSPortMessage defines a low level, operating-system independent type for interapplication (and interthread) messages. NSPortMessages are used primarily by the distributed objects system. You should implement interapplication communication using distributed objects whenever possible, and use NSPortMessages only when necessary.\
\fs14\
\fs28 An NSPortMessage has three major parts: the \i send\i0  and \i receive ports\i0 , which are NSPorts that link the sender of the message to the receiver, and the \i components\i0 , which form the body of the message. The components are held as an NSArray of NSData and NSPort objects. NSPortMessage\'27s \b sendBeforeDate:\b0  message sends the components out through the send port; any replies to the message arrive on the receive port. See the NSPort class specification for information on handling incoming messages.\
\fs14\
\fs28 An NSPortMessage can be initialized with a Mach message or with a pair of NSPorts and an NSArray of components. In either case, an NSPortMessage\'27s body can contain only the types in the table below. The types listed under \b initWithMachMessage:\b0  are converted to the corresponding object type listed under \b initWithSendPort:receivePort:components:\b0 . In the distributed objects system the byte/character arrays are usually encoded NSInvocations that are being forwarded from a proxy to the corresponding real object.\
\fs14\
\pard \s63 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx6753 \tx9553 \f0 \b \fs28 initWithMachMessage:\tab initWithSendPort:receivePort:components:\
